#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198096 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c23caadd-1738-549f-aade-2c983dc250dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of a Predicate"}] :question/id #uuid "4b253527-f5ca-5927-97f0-d91491d598b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12721" :question/problem "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P(x):$ $x^2+2x=3$\n\nwhere the universal set is $\\mathbb{Z}.$" :question/worked-solution "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x)$ is the set of elements in its universal set $U$ such that substituting these elements for the variable makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ x \\in U \\: | \\: P(x) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is $U = \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\nWe need to find $x \\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $x^2+2x=3$\n\nNow, we solve the above equation by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-3 & =0 \\\\ (x+3)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-3\\in\\mathbb Z$ and $x=1\\in\\mathbb Z.$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is $T = \\{-3,1\\}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198097 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa2f2ccb-45b0-5d6a-aa94-ac3eb95c745b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates of More Than One Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "14dc17f1-056e-5e82-81e6-c3964b7df2d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-12722" :question/problem "Which of the following are mathematical predicates?\n\nI. $x \\geq y$\n\nII. $\\dfrac{p}{q}$ is a rational number.\n\nIII. $a$ is a positive number." :question/worked-solution "A *predicate* of the variables $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ is a sentence that contains the variables defined on some *universal set,* and has the property that when we substitute concrete values for all the variables into the predicate, the result becomes a statement that is either true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our options. \n\n- Option I is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(x,y)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option II is a predicate of two variables. Substituting a concrete pair of values $(p,q)$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\n- Option III is a predicate of one variable. Substituting a concrete value for $a$ into the sentence, we get a statement that is either true or false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198098 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f9703bd-92ae-51f7-902b-251a7947ba6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of a Multivariable Predicate"}] :question/id #uuid "3d212cb2-394b-5f9d-8e7d-8406ec7ca580" :question/math-academy-id "e-12723" :question/problem "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$\\qquad$ $p \\, | \\, q$, \n\nwhere the universal set is $\\big\\{ (p,q) \\: | \\: p = 1,2,6, \\: q = 3,6,9 \\big\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "The *truth set* of a predicate $P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n)$ is the set of $n$-tuples in its universal set $U = U_1 \\times U_2 \\times \\cdots \\times U_n$ such that substituting these tuples for the variables makes the predicate a true statement:\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ (x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\in U \\: | \\: P(x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe universal set for our predicate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U & =\\{1,2,6\\}\\times \\{3,6,9\\} \\\\  & =\\{(1,3),(1,6),(1,9),(2,3),(2,6),(2,9),(6,3),(6,6),(6,9)\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find $(p,q) \\in U$ such that the following is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ $p \\, | \\, q$\n\nThe following pairs give the elements of our truth set:\n\n- $(1,3)$ since $1$ divides $3,$\n\n- $(1,6)$ since $1$ divides $6,$\n\n- $(1,9)$ since $1$ divides $9,$\n\n- $(2,6)$ since $2$ divides $6,$\n\n- $(6,6)$ since $6$ divides $6.$\n\nTherefore, the truth set of our predicate is\n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ (1,3), (1,6), (1,9), (2,6), (6,6) \\big\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198279 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c23caadd-1738-549f-aade-2c983dc250dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Truth Set of a Predicate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8406c8e-4b8a-577f-8f79-67a35136e4e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194827} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4c2a8c-657e-5d08-a0f4-c0b9f16561ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞, 16]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194828} {:answer/id #uuid "0633f29c-631e-59f4-be7f-78eba09a69d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(- ∞, 5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194829} {:answer/id #uuid "2a9e4124-b14c-5364-bc7e-ffd9fe20c2c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∅" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194830} {:answer/id #uuid "7f63d4ba-7fcf-55d4-b0be-ea206237086f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5, ∞)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194831}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8282d9e0-da0f-5db8-b1b4-4fe7082bb186" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbbd8880-0f23-5656-95b0-9fa4d1440eb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-171531" :question/problem "Find the truth set of the following predicate:\n\n$P(x)$: $3x + 1 \\le 16$,\n\nwhere the universal set is $R$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186575599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f9703bd-92ae-51f7-902b-251